{
 "measured": "2026-09-21",
 "what": "88 runs across 15 arms on the real 1.4B tower, in four phases, on shardset-v4 (the first shard set whose header pins corpus_rev, so shard_rev_matches reads Ran(passed=True) rather than NotRun). Every run is under the SEEDED QwenDecisionStep of fee0f9a; nothing on this box before that commit is comparable.",
 "why": "The night was planned as a 20-seed span_weight sweep -- the question AUDIT/ft-span-weight-seeded-2026-09-21.json left open. A smoke run against the regenerated shard set did not reproduce that file's seed-0 numbers, four runs at --seeds 0 0 1 1 showed two runs at ONE seed differing at step one, and the cause was that QwenDecisionStep seeded nothing. So phase 1 became: how much spread is left once the seed is honoured. Running a hundred sweep arms before knowing that would have measured the same mistake more precisely.",
 "host": "192.222.58.240, NVIDIA GH200 480GB, torch 2.10.0+cu128; 07:14-11:56 UTC",
 "backbone": "b1485b2fa6dfa1287294f269f5fb618e03d52d7c remapped to the corpus vocabulary; 1.4B trainable",
 "corpus": "/home/ubuntu/shardset-v4, 321 sequences, 78 span rows, data_snapshot_hash 101168a9005529a2 -- byte-identical rows to shardset-v3, the header gained the rev pin and nothing else. BOTH channel floors measure 0.0, so every gap below is optimisation rather than the corpus, and this is a memorisation regime with headroom a real task will not have.",
 "bar": "floor + 0.05, the tool's FLOOR_SLACK, unchanged (rule 2)",
 "ledger": "ledger/gh200-overnight-2026-09-21.jsonl, chain verifies at 176 rows (88 runs x an ft row and a verdict row). A ledger opened for this run rather than appended to a copied one, which is the direction GAP-LEDGER-NO-STORY-FOR-A-CHAIN-FORKED-ACROSS-TWO-MACHINES names; find_forks over ledger/*.jsonl now reports the known row-157 DIVERGENCE plus one ROOT fork, and the two are not the same thing.",
 "phase_1_the_residual": {
  "arm": "p1-residual-seed0-x8: one configuration, eight repeats at seed 0",
  "finding": "The seed now fixes the starting point and NOT the outcome. All eight opened at an identical total loss of 518.5886. The final span loss still ranged 0.000000 to 1.505752 with 3 of 8 over the bar -- the whole range the 3x3 had attributed to its three span_weight arms, produced by one arm at one seed. The final letter loss stayed inside 1.0e-5..1.3e-4, about 380x below its own bar.",
  "consequence": "At 128 steps the span channel is not measurable per seed, and n=12 does not fix that. The letter channel is."
 },
 "phase_2_the_sweep": {
  "arms": "5 span_weights x 12 seeds, bf16, 128 optimizer steps",
  "finding": "It reproduces NEITHER of the 3x3's two headline claims. The letter gate passes 12 of 12 at the shipped default of 1.0 -- against '2 of 3' -- and 11 of 12 at 0.5, which is the only arm that fails at all. Mean letter gap by span_weight is 0.00036, 0.00050, 0.00010, 0.01360, 0.00045 at 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1.0: no trend, monotone or otherwise. The span gate passes 9-11 of 12 everywhere with per-arm maxima of 0.69 to 2.94, which is phase 1's residual.",
  "consequence": "The 3x3's 'letter channel improves monotonically on every seed' and 'at the shipped default one seed in three fails the letter floor gate' were three draws each. Neither survives twelve."
 },
 "phase_3_the_optimizer_control": {
  "arms": "master at the SAME 128 steps, span_weight 1.0 and 0.05, 5 seeds each",
  "finding": "master at 128 steps is far WORSE than bf16 at 128: letter gate 2 of 5 at span_weight 1.0 and 1 of 5 at 0.05, against 12 of 12 for bf16, with mean letter gaps of 0.386 and 0.633 against 0.00045 and 0.00036.",
  "consequence": "This is the control that makes phase 4 attributable. Whatever closes the gap at 512 steps, it is not the master recipe -- at equal length the recipe costs."
 },
 "phase_4_the_long_schedule": {
  "arms": "master at 512 steps, span_weight 1.0 and 0.05, 5 seeds each",
  "finding": "BOTH arms reach BOTH floors on 5 of 5 seeds. At span_weight 1.0 the mean letter gap is 0.0000004 with a maximum of 0.00001, and the mean span gap 0.00003 with a maximum of 0.00012. At 0.05, 0.00001 and 0.00000. The span channel's spread collapses by four orders of magnitude against every 128-step arm.",
  "consequence": "The caveat the 3x3 left -- 'a longer schedule may close the 1.0 arm's gap on its own' -- is answered yes, and it closes BOTH channels. Most of phase 1's residual is an unconverged budget rather than nondeterministic kernels."
 },
 "what_this_settles": "span_weight's default STAYS AT 1.0, now for a positive reason rather than for absence of evidence: at a budget that converges, it reaches both floors on every seed, and at a budget that does not, the arms are not separable from the run-to-run residual. Two days of span_weight investigation were measuring how fast an unconverged run gets somewhere, per seed, with a spread larger than the effect.",
 "what_this_does_NOT_settle": [
  "Both floors are 0.0 on this corpus. A corpus with a nonzero floor is untested, and that is the regime where an objective's weighting could still matter.",
  "There is no bf16 arm at 512 steps, because build_optimizer refuses one past step 384. So phase 4 is 'master at 512', and 'bf16 at 512' is not a run that may be made -- which is the guard working, not a gap in the design.",
  "Phase 1 bounds the residual at 128 steps and phase 4 bounds it at 512. Neither measures how much of the 128-step spread is kernels rather than convergence; --deterministic now exists to ask that and was not run in this set.",
  "Every row is quick=True and promotes nothing (rule 8)."
 ],
